0006-ptr-cuda-dmabuf-and-flush.patch
===================================================================
nccl-mesh-plugin @ 19924dcc, on top of 0004 and 0005.
Files: src/mesh_plugin.c, include/mesh_plugin.h.  No wire-format change.

WHAT IT FIXES
-------------
getProperties advertised ptrSupport = NCCL_PTR_HOST only, so NCCL never handed
this plugin a GPU pointer and staged every transfer through a cudaHostAlloc
bounce buffer - NCCL logs it as "GPU Direct RDMA Disabled for HCA 0..3".
Meanwhile regMr had already been written to register a CUDA pointer directly
(the TICKET-C comment describes exactly that), and the platform supports it:
GB10 has no separate framebuffer, Addressing Mode is ATS, C2C is on, and one
LPDDR5X pool serves both processors, so a cudaMalloc pointer is an ordinary
virtual address whose pages the CPU can also reach.  The only thing standing in
the way was the advertisement.

WHAT IT DOES
------------
1. ptrSupport comes from a new mesh_ptr_support():
       NCCL_PTR_HOST
     | NCCL_PTR_CUDA    when NCCL_MESH_PTR_CUDA != 0            (default 1)
     | NCCL_PTR_DMABUF  when NCCL_MESH_DMABUF != 0 AND ibv_reg_dmabuf_mr
                        actually resolves at runtime            (default 0)
   Applied to both getProperties (v8) and getProperties_v9 (the one in use).

2. regMr and regMrDmaBuf now share mesh_reg_mr_common(), which tries, in order:
     a. ibv_reg_dmabuf_mr with the fd NCCL supplied (regMrDmaBuf path only);
     b. plain ibv_reg_mr - the expected path on this part;
     c. for a device/managed pointer ibv_reg_mr refused: export a DMA-BUF with
        cuMemGetHandleForAddressRange(CU_MEM_RANGE_HANDLE_TYPE_DMA_BUF_FD) and
        register that.  The fd is closed immediately afterwards - the kernel
        takes its own reference on the dma_buf, which is what NCCL's own IB
        plugin relies on.
   Addresses are page-aligned down and the length rounded up for the DMA-BUF
   calls, mirroring ncclIbRegMrDmaBuf.  ibv_reg_dmabuf_mr and
   cuMemGetHandleForAddressRange are resolved with dlsym, so the plugin still
   loads on a libibverbs without DMA-BUF and never pulls CUDA in itself.
   A registration failure is now loud and fatal instead of being described as
   "NCCL will use host staging buffers": with NCCL_PTR_CUDA advertised there is
   no host buffer to fall back to.  The warning names NCCL_MESH_PTR_CUDA=0.

   WHY DMABUF IS OFF BY DEFAULT.  regMrDmaBuf is genuinely implemented, so
   advertising the bit is honest, but on a unified-memory part the plain
   ibv_reg_mr path is the one that matches how the memory is actually backed.
   A DMA-BUF registration that succeeds while describing the wrong pages would
   corrupt data silently rather than fail - the same class of trap as the b12x
   22-head decode kernel.  So it is opt-in and gets its own A/B arm.

3. mesh_iflush stops being a no-op.  It posts a 4-byte IBV_WR_RDMA_READ of the
   last non-empty received buffer over a QP looped back to the same NIC, and
   returns a normal mesh_request that mesh_test polls - the same construction
   NCCL's own IB plugin uses (ncclIbIflush / gpuFlush).  The loopback QP, its
   CQ, the landing pad and its MR are built lazily on the first flush that
   needs one and destroyed in closeRecv.

   WHY NOT KEEP THE NO-OP.  The honest answer is that the coherence argument
   for GB10 is plausible but unmeasured: the NIC's data writes and its CQE
   write travel the same PCIe path, and GPU and CPU share the memory, so the
   ordering hazard the flush exists for may well not exist here.  "May well
   not" is not a proof, and an unflushed GDR read fails silently, so the
   default is the correct-by-construction one and the cheaper one is a knob to
   be measured:  NCCL_MESH_FLUSH=0 restores the no-op.  A flush that cannot be
   set up returns ncclSystemError rather than skipping quietly.
   Host pointers short-circuit before any of this, so with
   NCCL_MESH_PTR_CUDA=0 iflush behaves exactly as it did before.

4. mesh_init_cuda_check() also tries libcudart.so.13 / .11 and finally
   dlsym(RTLD_DEFAULT, ...).  The old code tried only libcudart.so and .so.12,
   and this stack runs NCCL 2.30.7+cuda13.3 - so pointer-kind detection could
   silently have been off the whole time.  It now logs which way it went, and
   when the CUDA runtime is unreachable it falls back to the pointer type NCCL
   itself passes to regMr.

EXPECTED EFFECT AND THE THING TO WATCH
--------------------------------------
This is not classic GPUDirect: there is no BAR1 window here and nvidia-peermem
solves a problem this platform does not have.  What it removes is NCCL's host
bounce buffer - one memory-to-memory copy, one proxy synchronisation point, and
the GPU's write penalty into host-mapped memory - and it is on top of, not
instead of, the idle-cable fix in 0005.

Turning GDR on changes NCCL's topology model, so the channel count that was
tuned against the host path (NCCL_MAX_NCHANNELS=8) is no longer known to be
right.  Re-sweep the channel cap in the same session.  Report the KV pool line
too: if NCCL's buffers move from host to device, the accounting under
gpu-memory-utilization moves with them.

KNOBS
-----
  NCCL_MESH_PTR_CUDA   1 = advertise NCCL_PTR_CUDA (default)
                       0 = host-only, exactly the pre-patch plugin
  NCCL_MESH_DMABUF     0 = do not advertise NCCL_PTR_DMABUF (default)
                       1 = advertise it, if ibv_reg_dmabuf_mr resolves
  NCCL_MESH_FLUSH      1 = real RDMA_READ flush (default)
                       0 = no-op flush, as before

TESTS RUN
---------
  make test-unit -> test_routing 13/13.
  test_error_paths -> 60 passed / 6 failed, byte-identical to the stock tree.
  Build clean under -Wall -Wextra.
  No fabric test, no engine boot, and no CUDA pointer has been registered yet:
  whether ibv_reg_mr accepts a cudaMalloc pointer on this part is still an
  open measurement, and it is the first thing the quiet-window plan checks.
===================================================================

